1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,610
Could we make passengers in a plane
or car feel no acceleration at all?

2
00:00:05,317 --> 00:00:09,627
The vehicle could speed up. It
could slow down. It could turn.

3
00:00:09,957 --> 00:00:13,187
And the people inside would
feel absolutely nothing.

4
00:00:13,972 --> 00:00:15,602
No pushing into the seat.

5
00:00:15,792 --> 00:00:18,922
No being thrown forward
when the vehicle brakes.

6
00:00:19,292 --> 00:00:21,482
And no sudden jolts during turbulence.

7
00:00:24,012 --> 00:00:26,102
But this is not only about comfort.

8
00:00:27,152 --> 00:00:28,582
Acceleration is a ceiling.

9
00:00:29,401 --> 00:00:32,991
A fighter pilot wears a suit that
squeezes the legs and the belly.

10
00:00:33,541 --> 00:00:36,691
And the pilot strains against
the force the whole time.

11
00:00:37,371 --> 00:00:42,141
Together, that buys about nine
times gravity. For tens of seconds.

12
00:00:43,079 --> 00:00:46,989
Without that, a few times gravity is
enough to drain the blood from your head.

13
00:00:47,799 --> 00:00:50,969
Your vision greys out. Then you black out.

14
00:00:51,819 --> 00:00:55,569
So every vehicle that carries people
is built around a very low limit.

15
00:00:56,259 --> 00:00:59,949
Not the limit of the engine.
The limit of the passenger.

16
00:01:02,759 --> 00:01:06,049
This sounds like science
fiction. And it is.

17
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:09,749
But the physics behind it
is surprisingly interesting.

18
00:01:10,559 --> 00:01:13,349
So what would we actually have
to invent to make it possible?

19
00:01:14,275 --> 00:01:17,365
Let's start with the simplest
problem: Acceleration.

20
00:01:18,381 --> 00:01:22,451
When a car accelerates, your body wants
to keep moving at its original speed.

21
00:01:23,461 --> 00:01:24,971
The seat pushes you forward.

22
00:01:25,721 --> 00:01:28,351
That force accelerates your
body along with the car.

23
00:01:29,105 --> 00:01:31,275
And you feel that force as acceleration.

24
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:34,490
When the car brakes, the opposite happens.

25
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,710
The seat belt stops you.

26
00:01:37,427 --> 00:01:40,067
So if we want you to
feel no acceleration, we

27
00:01:40,147 --> 00:01:42,997
somehow have to prevent that
force from reaching you.

28
00:01:45,417 --> 00:01:48,187
One obvious solution would
be to move your seat.

29
00:01:49,097 --> 00:01:51,357
When the plane accelerates
forward, the seat

30
00:01:51,437 --> 00:01:54,007
could move backward at
exactly the right speed.

31
00:01:54,902 --> 00:01:58,372
From your point of view, you would
remain almost perfectly still.

32
00:01:59,094 --> 00:02:00,624
This is not science fiction.

33
00:02:01,534 --> 00:02:04,404
A flight simulator sits
on six moving legs.

34
00:02:05,094 --> 00:02:07,244
It moves well enough to train real pilots.

35
00:02:08,239 --> 00:02:10,069
But look at what it actually does.

36
00:02:10,839 --> 00:02:13,089
The legs give it a metre or so of room.

37
00:02:13,499 --> 00:02:15,849
So the platform cannot keep accelerating.

38
00:02:16,958 --> 00:02:20,668
When the pilot opens the throttle,
the simulator tilts backwards.

39
00:02:21,683 --> 00:02:24,173
And gravity pushes the
pilot into the seat.

40
00:02:24,943 --> 00:02:26,853
The inner ear cannot tell the difference.

41
00:02:27,797 --> 00:02:30,627
So the simulator is not
cancelling acceleration.

42
00:02:31,677 --> 00:02:34,067
It is borrowing gravity to imitate it.

43
00:02:34,765 --> 00:02:36,975
Remember that. We will come back to it.

44
00:02:39,625 --> 00:02:43,775
But back to the moving seat. There
is a problem. A very big one.

45
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:48,110
Suppose the vehicle accelerates
at one G for just ten seconds.

46
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:51,210
That's roughly the
acceleration of gravity.

47
00:02:52,215 --> 00:02:55,095
After ten seconds, the vehicle
would be moving about three

48
00:02:55,175 --> 00:02:58,785
hundred and fifty kilometres
per hour faster than before.

49
00:02:59,629 --> 00:03:02,269
To keep you stationary,
your seat would have to move

50
00:03:02,349 --> 00:03:05,239
almost five hundred metres
relative to the vehicle.

51
00:03:05,997 --> 00:03:10,727
So our first inertial damper would need
a seat rail almost half a kilometre long.

52
00:03:11,490 --> 00:03:14,120
Probably not very practical
for economy class.

53
00:03:14,870 --> 00:03:16,480
So let's try something else.

54
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:21,690
What if we could push
the passenger directly?

55
00:03:22,619 --> 00:03:24,709
Sensors could measure the acceleration.

56
00:03:25,859 --> 00:03:28,209
A computer could work
out the force required.

57
00:03:28,699 --> 00:03:32,469
And an actuator could push your
body in exactly the right direction.

58
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:34,710
That sounds much better.

59
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:37,450
But there is still a fundamental problem.

60
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:39,990
Where does that force come from?

61
00:03:40,653 --> 00:03:42,833
If you push the passenger
forward, something

62
00:03:42,913 --> 00:03:45,523
else has to receive the
corresponding reaction.

63
00:03:46,488 --> 00:03:48,628
We haven't eliminated inertia — the

64
00:03:48,708 --> 00:03:51,218
resistance of matter
to changing its motion.

65
00:03:52,248 --> 00:03:53,718
We are simply fighting it.

66
00:03:54,437 --> 00:03:57,947
And this is where the science fiction
version gets really interesting.

67
00:03:58,917 --> 00:04:01,387
What if we could somehow
change inertia itself?

68
00:04:03,927 --> 00:04:05,787
Imagine a technology
that could temporarily

69
00:04:05,867 --> 00:04:08,617
change how matter
responds to acceleration.

70
00:04:09,847 --> 00:04:13,397
The vehicle could accelerate.
But your body would not have to.

71
00:04:14,051 --> 00:04:16,121
That would be a real inertial damper.

72
00:04:16,231 --> 00:04:20,341
Not a better suspension
system. Not a clever seat.

73
00:04:20,751 --> 00:04:22,561
Something fundamentally new.

74
00:04:22,851 --> 00:04:25,301
And we don't currently
know how to do that.

75
00:04:28,001 --> 00:04:30,591
But now think about a
force you have never felt.

76
00:04:31,447 --> 00:04:32,647
Gravity.

77
00:04:33,569 --> 00:04:37,139
Right now, gravity is pulling you
towards the centre of the Earth.

78
00:04:37,932 --> 00:04:39,422
And you cannot feel it.

79
00:04:40,289 --> 00:04:42,399
What you feel is the chair under you.

80
00:04:42,989 --> 00:04:45,679
Take the chair away, and
the feeling goes with it.

81
00:04:46,339 --> 00:04:48,569
In free fall, you feel nothing at all.

82
00:04:49,379 --> 00:04:52,329
Astronauts do not float
because gravity is gone.

83
00:04:52,779 --> 00:04:55,509
They float because they
are falling continuously.

84
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:58,910
And here is why that matters so much.

85
00:04:59,749 --> 00:05:01,559
A seat pushes you in one place.

86
00:05:02,389 --> 00:05:06,419
That push then has to travel through
your body. Your bones compress.

87
00:05:06,649 --> 00:05:10,199
Your organs shift. And that
is what you actually feel.

88
00:05:10,945 --> 00:05:12,795
Gravity does not do that.

89
00:05:13,345 --> 00:05:15,815
It gives every part of
you the same acceleration.

90
00:05:16,952 --> 00:05:18,322
Nothing is compressed.

91
00:05:18,432 --> 00:05:19,862
So there is nothing to feel.

92
00:05:20,696 --> 00:05:24,586
Einstein built general relativity
on exactly that observation.

93
00:05:25,596 --> 00:05:27,606
A falling person feels no gravity.

94
00:05:28,538 --> 00:05:30,748
So the goal is not to cancel a force.

95
00:05:31,618 --> 00:05:34,948
The goal is to accelerate every
part of you by the same amount.

96
00:05:35,825 --> 00:05:38,435
Which is precisely what
gravity already does.

97
00:05:38,525 --> 00:05:41,735
And it is why the
simulator could borrow it.

98
00:05:44,665 --> 00:05:47,295
So what if we could
manipulate gravity itself?

99
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:52,730
In Einstein's general relativity,
gravity is not really a force.

100
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:57,330
Matter and energy curve spacetime,
and things move through that curve.

101
00:05:58,129 --> 00:06:00,789
So if we could control
spacetime itself, we

102
00:06:00,869 --> 00:06:03,119
would not have to push
the passenger at all.

103
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:06,839
We would change the geometry
the passenger moves through.

104
00:06:07,592 --> 00:06:10,202
From inside the vehicle, it
would look almost magical.

105
00:06:11,232 --> 00:06:13,442
The ship accelerates at enormous speed.

106
00:06:13,692 --> 00:06:15,962
And inside, nothing is felt.

107
00:06:16,844 --> 00:06:18,824
This is roughly the kind of idea that

108
00:06:18,904 --> 00:06:21,534
science fiction calls
an inertial dampener.

109
00:06:22,231 --> 00:06:23,431
But there is a catch.

110
00:06:25,741 --> 00:06:28,511
We can measure gravity with
extraordinary precision.

111
00:06:29,441 --> 00:06:33,251
We can even detect gravitational
waves from colliding black holes.

112
00:06:34,051 --> 00:06:36,871
But we have no technology
that can turn it down.

113
00:06:37,617 --> 00:06:41,147
We don't even know whether gravity
has a quantum particle at all.

114
00:06:41,877 --> 00:06:43,887
Physicists call it the graviton.

115
00:06:44,257 --> 00:06:46,547
Nobody has ever detected one.

116
00:06:49,207 --> 00:06:52,457
So could we really build a
science-fiction inertial damper?

117
00:06:53,164 --> 00:06:54,854
With today's physics: No.

118
00:06:57,674 --> 00:07:00,854
But if we someday learn to
manipulate inertia, gravity, or

119
00:07:00,934 --> 00:07:04,744
spacetime itself, the situation
could change completely.

120
00:07:05,621 --> 00:07:08,061
Because then the problem
would no longer be: "How

121
00:07:08,141 --> 00:07:10,571
do we make the vehicle
accelerate more gently?"

122
00:07:11,421 --> 00:07:15,491
It would become: "How do we make the
passengers not share that acceleration?"

123
00:07:16,641 --> 00:07:18,691
And that distinction could be enormous.

124
00:07:18,821 --> 00:07:21,501
Because a spacecraft that cannot hurt its

125
00:07:21,581 --> 00:07:24,291
passengers no longer has
to crawl through space.

126
00:07:25,230 --> 00:07:30,400
You could keep accelerating. And
accelerating. And accelerating.

127
00:07:31,010 --> 00:07:35,260
Until the spacecraft reaches a significant
fraction of the speed of light.

128
00:07:36,084 --> 00:07:39,874
The hardest part of interstellar travel
might not be reaching a high speed.

129
00:07:40,584 --> 00:07:44,054
It might be surviving the
acceleration required to get there.

130
00:07:46,734 --> 00:07:49,624
So the inertial damper is
more than science fiction.

131
00:07:50,734 --> 00:07:54,104
It is a question about one of the
most basic properties of matter:

132
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:58,330
Why does matter resist being
accelerated in the first place?

133
00:07:59,131 --> 00:08:01,421
We know how to describe that behaviour.

134
00:08:02,251 --> 00:08:05,361
But we still don't have a technology
that lets us switch it off.

135
00:08:06,119 --> 00:08:07,489
Maybe we never will.

136
00:08:10,339 --> 00:08:13,979
Or maybe one day, the thing that
sounds most impossible will turn

137
00:08:14,059 --> 00:08:17,869
out to be the missing technology
that makes the stars reachable.
